==English==

===Etymology===
From {{suffix|en|absurd|ly}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/əbˈsɜːd.li/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/æbˈsɝd.li/|/æbˈzɝd.li/|/əbˈsɝd.li/|/əbˈzɝd.li/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-absurdly.ogg|a=US}}

===Adverb===
{{en-adv}}

# In an [[absurd]] fashion. {{defdate|First attested in the mid 16<sup>th</sup> century.}}<ref name=SOED>{{R:SOED5|page=10}}</ref>
#: {{ux|en|He orated '''absurdly'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Absurdly''', he concluded his oration with a song.}}
# To an extreme degree. {{defdate|First attested in the mid 16<sup>th</sup> century.}}<ref name=SOED/>
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1996|author=Jon Byrell|title=Lairs, Urgers and Coat-Tuggers|publisher=Ironbark|location=Sydney|page=245|passage=By the year 1765, Richard `Dickie' Tattersall, the '''absurdly''' romantic self-styled soldier of fortune and indulged son of an obscenely well-off Yorkshire squire, was brimming with gratitude[.]}}
#: {{ux|en|The critics were '''absurdly''' extravagant with their praise.}}

